#!/usr/bin/env perl

package Trog::Bin::SetupProvisioner;

use 5.041;

use strict;
use warnings FATAL => 'all';

use re '/aa';

# Basically our 'install script'

#TODO ideally, we just ship this all as a 'config rpm/deb'.

=head1 NAME

setup_provisioner - prepare this machine to be a hypervisor

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    bin/setup_provisioner

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The install script: everything a machine needs before it can host guests.

Every step is still a stub: each returns true and says what it ought to be
doing.  Each step's number is what main() returns when that step fails.

There used to be an rsyslog step here, which opened a syslog listener by editing
/etc/rsyslog.conf on whatever machine you ran this from -- while every guest was
told to ship its logs to the hypervisor, which is a different machine on any
fleet with more than one.  Where a guest sends its logs is now
Provisioner::Recipe::logshipper, and the listener at the other end is
Provisioner::Recipe::logcollector, so there is nothing for this to set up.

=cut

sub main {
    my @args = @_;

    #    GetOptionsFromArray(\@args,
    #
    #    );

    setup_kvm()                  or return 1;
    setup_public_bridge()        or return 2;
    setup_auditd()               or return 3;
    setup_management_interface() or return 4;
    setup_firewall()             or return 5;
    setup_fail2ban()             or return 6;
    setup_crons()                or return 7;
    return 0;
}

